\1\n    <div id="site-header"></div>
    <!-- Header -->
    
    <main class="lesson-container py-4">
        <!-- Breadcrumb -->
        <div id="tutorial-header"></div>
        
        <div class="content lesson-content" style="font-size: 1.05rem; line-height: 1.7; color: var(--text-secondary);">

            <p><strong>Network latency</strong> is the time it takes for data to travel from source to destination. In troubleshooting, we typically measure Round Trip Time (RTT)—the time taken for a packet to go and the response to come back.</p>
            
            <h3>Components of Latency</h3>
            <ul>
                <li><strong>Propagation Delay:</strong> The speed of light through fiber or copper. Distance inherently adds latency.</li>
                <li><strong>Serialization / Transmission Delay:</strong> The time required to clock bits onto the physical wire.</li>
                <li><strong>Queuing Delay:</strong> Time spent waiting in a router buffer during congestion.</li>
                <li><strong>Processing Delay:</strong> Time taken by devices to inspect headers, encrypt packets (VPN overhead), or make routing decisions.</li>
            </ul>
            
            <h3>Troubleshooting Workflow</h3>
            <p>Because latency, bandwidth, and <a href="packet-loss-troubleshooting.html">packet loss</a> are different metrics, you must isolate the latency source:</p>
            <ol>
                <li>Ping your local gateway (expected &lt;1ms on LAN, slightly higher on Wi-Fi due to retries/interference).</li>
                <li>Ping an internal service.</li>
                <li>Ping an Internet destination.</li>
                <li>Use <code>tracert</code> or <code>pathping</code> to visualize path delay.</li>
                <li>Use an application port test (e.g., TCP ping) to rule out ICMP-specific delays.</li>
            </ol>
            
            <h3>Important Distinctions</h3>
            <p>Avoid applying absolute thresholds like "anything above 50 ms is bad" without context. 50 ms is terrible for a local database connection, but excellent for a trans-Atlantic WAN link.</p>
            <p>Furthermore, distinguish between network latency and slow server/application performance. If a ping returns in 10ms but a web page takes 10 seconds to load, the network is likely fine; the server is slow.</p>
            <p>Finally, as with packet loss, latency observed in a traceroute at an intermediate hop may not mean that hop is actually delaying forwarded traffic; routers often deprioritize generating ICMP Time Exceeded responses.</p>
        
        </div>
        
